From: Arif Alam <arif.alam@netexperience.com>
Date: Thu, 28 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath12k: resolve peer by vdev_id in peer delete path

ath12k_peer_delete_send() resolves the peer it is about to tear down with
ath12k_peer_find_by_addr(), which is a lookup in the MAC-address keyed
rhashtable. That table is shared by all pdevs of an ath12k_base, so on a
split-PHY device (2 GHz + 5 GHz on one SoC) it holds exactly one entry per
client address, no matter which radio the client is on.

When a client roams from one band to another, ath12k_peer_create() on the
new radio replaces the rhash entry with the new link peer before mac80211
removes the station from the old radio. The subsequent delete on the old
vdev then finds the *new* peer by address. The vdev_id check only guards
the rhash removal - the rest of the function keeps operating on that
wrongly resolved peer, so it:

  - clears ab->peer_list[] at the *new* peer's peer_id, which permanently
    removes the roamed client from the RCU peer lookup used by the Rx fast
    path (ath12k_peer_find_by_peer_list_index() in ath12k_dp_rx_h_mpdu()).
    Every uplink frame from that client then falls back to the slow path:
    ab->base_lock is taken per packet, the peer is re-resolved from
    addr2 in the Rx descriptor, PPE/SFE direct delivery is skipped and the
    frame is pushed through the full mac80211 Rx stack. The result is a
    large, permanent uplink throughput drop for the roamed client.

  - leaves ab->peer_list[] populated at the *old* peer's peer_id. The old
    peer is freed shortly after by ath12k_peer_unmap_event(), which relies
    on this function having cleared the slot and having done the
    synchronize_rcu(), so a dangling pointer is left behind in the Rx
    lookup table.

Resolve the peer with ath12k_peer_find(), which is keyed by (vdev_id,
addr), so the delete path always acts on the peer belonging to the vdev
being torn down. Keep the address-keyed lookup only for deciding whether
the rhash entry is ours to remove - if it points at another radio's peer
it must be left alone, since that peer is the live one.

Signed-off-by: Arif Alam <arif.alam@netexperience.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/peer.c |  9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/peer.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/peer.c
@@ -567,8 +567,14 @@
 	if (peer && peer->vdev_id == vdev_id)
 		ath12k_peer_rhash_delete(ab, peer);
 
-	if (!peer)
-		peer = ath12k_peer_find(ab, vdev_id, addr);
+	/* The address keyed rhash table is shared by all pdevs of this
+	 * ath12k_base. On a cross band roam the entry for this address may
+	 * already have been replaced by the link peer created on the other
+	 * radio, and that peer must not be touched here. Always resolve the
+	 * peer being deleted by (vdev_id, addr) so that the peer list and ML
+	 * peer list slots cleared below belong to the vdev being torn down.
+	 */
+	peer = ath12k_peer_find(ab, vdev_id, addr);
 
 	if (!peer) {
 		spin_unlock_bh(&ab->base_lock);
